Dialogue: 0,0:00:01.80,0:00:06.37,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}A basic logistic regression separates\Nadults with and without diabetes
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.37,0:00:08.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}at an AUC of 0.961.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.85,0:00:13.30,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}From survey data alone,\Nthat is suspiciously high.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.30,0:00:16.70,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}I'm [Name];\NI led governance and integration.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.70,0:00:19.55,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}[Name] led data quality and preparation,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.55,0:00:24.41,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}[Name] exploration, labels and bias,\Nand [Name] modelling and conclusions.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.41,0:00:28.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}We present in that order,\Nand the 0.961 runs through the whole talk.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.30,0:00:34.88,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Our question: who gets labelled diabetic,\Nand what do preparation decisions change?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:34.88,0:00:40.28,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The design: one L2 logistic regression\Non three preparations of the same data,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:40.28,0:00:42.52,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}naive, minimal and systematic,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.52,0:00:45.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}scored on all adults\Nand on adults not yet diagnosed.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:46.95,0:00:50.23,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Our research questions:\Nwhat does the label measure?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.23,0:00:52.35,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Is the high AUC leakage?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:52.35,0:00:54.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Does systematic preparation pay?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:55.30,0:00:59.85,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The data: NHANES 2021 to 2023,\Nthe CDC's national health survey.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:59.85,0:01:04.41,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Our cohort is 5,730 non-pregnant adults\Nwith an HbA1c result.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:04.41,0:01:07.02,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The label is positive\Nfor a self-reported diagnosis,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.02,0:01:09.73,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}or HbA1c of 6.5 percent or more.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:09.73,0:01:12.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Survey-weighted,\N14.0 percent are positive.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:14.30,0:01:18.20,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Governance came first.\NThe NCHS data use agreement forbids
Dialogue: 0,0:01:18.20,0:01:21.66,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}re-identification and linkage,\Nso we did neither.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.66,0:01:27.20,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}We kept 78 of 175 columns,\Nverified every raw file by hash,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:27.20,0:01:31.10,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}and locked our hypotheses by hash\Nbefore running any model.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.30,0:01:36.23,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}To check our survey-weighting code,\Nwe re-ran NCHS Data Brief 516:
Dialogue: 0,0:01:36.23,0:01:40.14,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}total prevalence 15.79 percent\Nagainst the published 15.8,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.14,0:01:42.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}and all 27 published\Nestimates match closely.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.30,0:01:46.33,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Correct numbers can still mislead.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:46.33,0:01:47.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}In [Name]'s analysis,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:47.90,0:01:52.57,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}insured adults show 5.3 points\Nmore diabetes than the uninsured.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.57,0:01:56.32,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}That is confounding by age:\Nthe insured are much older.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:56.32,0:01:57.49,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Age-standardised,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.49,0:02:01.70,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}the gap falls to 0.7 points,\Nand its interval includes zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:03.30,0:02:09.48,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Most unexpected for me: of six hypotheses\Nlocked before we saw any related data,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:09.48,0:02:11.17,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}only three were supported.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.42,0:02:15.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Now [Name] asks whether\Nthe data deserve the same trust.
